<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jutes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jutes were a Germanic people who, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> and settled in Kent, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a> in the 5th century, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. Kent's royal house traced its origin to the Jutish leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hengist-and-horsa">Hengist and Horsa</a>. Archaeology shows close links between early Kent, Jutland and Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and the Jutes soon merged into the wider English population.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jutes.jpg" alt="Jutes" width="320" height="578" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LibIchtnatz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jutland_Peninsula_map.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, the Jutes were one of the three most powerful Germanic nations that came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, along with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. From them, he wrote, descended the people of Kent, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a> and those in the West Saxon province opposite the island. Historians have no consensus on their origins. Bede inferred a homeland on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> peninsula, and one hypothesis holds that they left Jutland after a Danish invasion and moved to the Frisian coast. Analysis of grave goods, however, links east Kent, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a> and the Isle of Wight to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> more than to Jutland, and some sources seem to use the names Frisians and Jutes interchangeably.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, which historians regard as giving foundation legends, says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hengist-and-horsa">Hengist and Horsa</a> came in 449 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vortigern">Vortigern</a>'s invitation to help against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> and landed at Ebbsfleet; later settlers came from the Old Saxons, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> and the Jutes, who took Kent, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>. It names Wihtgar and Stuf as founders of the Isle of Wight and Port with his sons as founders of the Meonwara in southern Hampshire. Contemporary writing is scarce before the 7th century, and some archaeological findings challenge the Chronicle's early dates. The Jutes probably settled Kent first and spread from there.</p><p>In 676 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-of-mercia">Æthelred of Mercia</a> ravaged Kent and destroyed Rochester. In 681 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wulfhere-of-mercia">Wulfhere of Mercia</a> advanced into southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a>, which he gave to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelwealh-of-sussex">Æthelwealh of Sussex</a>. In the 680s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caedwalla">Cædwalla of Wessex</a> conquered Sussex and the Jutish lands in Hampshire and the Isle of Wight. Bede says he killed the island's king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arwald">Arwald</a>, and tried to destroy its inhabitants and replace them with people from his own province, but that Jutes remained the majority there. The island was then permanently under West Saxon control and the Meonwara joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>. Kent kept its independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Brooches and bracteates found in east Kent, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a> show strong Frankish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> influence from the mid-fifth to the late sixth century. Kent was culturally distinct east from west until the late 6th century, when west Kent adopted east Kent's material culture. The Frankish princess Bertha came to Kent around 580 to marry King Æthelberht, bringing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a> sent Augustine in 597. The Isle of Wight was the last area of Anglo-Saxon England to be converted, in 686. The Jutes practised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partible-inheritance">partible inheritance</a> known as gavelkind, which was kept in Kent until its abolition in 1925.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jutes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saxon settlers, with Angles and <b>Jutes</b>, founded the kingdoms of the Anglo-Saxons, and the names Essex, Sussex and Wessex preserve the East, South and West Saxons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Old English developed from a set of Anglo-Frisian or Ingvaeonic dialects, part of the North Sea Germanic group of West Germanic, spoken by the Germanic tribes traditionally known as the Angles, Saxons and <b>Jutes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Bede says Kent was settled by <b>Jutes</b> led by Hengest and Horsa, who came as mercenaries of Vortigern around 449 and then turned against the Britons.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kent">Kingdom of Kent</a></li><li><blockquote>Bede reports that the Isle of Wight, like Kent and part of Hampshire, was settled by <b>Jutes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-isle-of-wight">Kingdom of the Isle of Wight</a></li></ul></section>
